Composition over inheritance.

You've now seen both ways to build a class on top of others: inheritance, with extends, an is-a relationship; and implementing interfaces. This closing section is about a design instinct that quietly shapes good Java. When you need one object's behaviour inside another, prefer composition — holding an object as a field — over inheritance — becoming a subclass of it.

Let's name the two relationships clearly. Inheritance is "is-a". A savings account is a bank account, so it extends bank account. Composition is "has-a". A car has an engine; an order service has a repository. The outer object keeps the other one as a field, and delegates to it. So an order service holds a repository, and its place-order method simply calls save on that repository. It has-a repository, and it delegates the work.

So why does inheritance so often disappoint as a tool for reuse? Because it comes with strings attached. First, it's rigid. You inherit everything the parent exposes, wanted or not, and you're locked to a single superclass forever. Second, it's fragile. Because a subclass leans on the parent's internals, an innocent change upstream can silently break it — that's the classic fragile base class problem. And third, it leaks the parent's whole surface. Extend an array list to make a stack, and you've also exposed add, and remove-at-index, and everything else — so callers can just bypass your rules. Composition avoids all three, because you hold the collaborator behind your own interface, and expose only the methods you choose.

The classic example is exactly that stack. Instead of extending a list, you hold one. Your stack has a private array list inside it, and it exposes exactly three methods: push, which adds to the list; pop, which removes the last element; and is-empty. There's no stray add-at-index to corrupt it. And because the list is hidden, you can later swap the array list for a linked list, and not a single caller notices. That's the flexibility inheritance simply can't give you.

So here's the guideline. Use inheritance for a true, stable is-a relationship with real shared behaviour — and keep those hierarchies shallow. Use composition for everything else, which in practice is most of the time. It couples loosely, hides internals, and lets you change your mind later. Pair it with the earlier habit — program to interfaces — and you get the reusable, testable shape that modern Java aims for: small objects that hold their collaborators, through composition, and depend on their capabilities, through interfaces, rather than on a tall tree of extends. That instinct — has-a over is-a — is the quiet backbone of good object-oriented design, and it closes out the module.
